WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.127
Secure Go service architecture begins with
clear boundaries and explicit

2
00:00:04.207 --> 00:00:04.898
assumptions.

3
00:00:04.978 --> 00:00:08.259
A team should know who can call the
service, what the service trusts,

4
00:00:08.339 --> 00:00:11.850
which dependencies it relies on, and where
security decisions are made.

5
00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:15.960
When boundaries are visible, controls
become easier to place and easier to review.

6
00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.458
Identify what data enters and leaves the
service,

7
00:00:18.538 --> 00:00:20.438
and which actions need authorization.

8
00:00:20.518 --> 00:00:23.511
Validate external input before business
logic depends on it.

9
00:00:23.591 --> 00:00:25.721
Normalize data into safer internal models.

10
00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:29.658
Limit outputs so callers receive what they
need without accidental exposure of

11
00:00:29.738 --> 00:00:31.638
internal fields or sensitive records.

12
00:00:31.718 --> 00:00:35.690
Protect secrets, use safe configuration
practices, and minimize dependencies.

13
00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:39.281
Configuration should be validated before
the service handles traffic.

14
00:00:39.361 --> 00:00:42.355
Secrets should stay out of source code,
logs, client bundles,

15
00:00:42.435 --> 00:00:44.392
and ordinary string handling patterns.

16
00:00:44.472 --> 00:00:48.272
Dependencies and build artifacts should be
reviewed, versioned, traceable,

17
00:00:48.352 --> 00:00:49.157
and maintained.

18
00:00:49.237 --> 00:00:52.519
Design for failure with timeouts,
cancellation, careful retries,

19
00:00:52.599 --> 00:00:55.017
resource limits, and meaningful error
handling.

20
00:00:55.097 --> 00:00:58.032
Go's context patterns help when they are
used consistently.

21
00:00:58.112 --> 00:01:01.048
Defensive services fail safely, preserve
useful evidence,

22
00:01:01.128 --> 00:01:04.122
and avoid exposing internal details that
callers do not need.

23
00:01:04.202 --> 00:01:08.232
Make behavior observable through useful
logs, metrics, traces, and audit events.

24
00:01:08.312 --> 00:01:12.111
Incident readiness depends on evidence
quality before something goes wrong.

25
00:01:12.191 --> 00:01:14.494
Good architecture is not security
decoration.

26
00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:18.028
It is practical engineering that makes
safer behavior easier to build,

27
00:01:18.108 --> 00:01:20.180
maintain, operate, and improve over time.
